WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.350
Guess my number, I told my

2
00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:04.830
class: from one to a hundred.

3
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.660
Half of them guessed one, two,

4
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.180
three, four, and gave up.

5
00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.530
One girl said fifty, then

6
00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.890
twenty-five, and found it in six guesses.

7
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.250
Today: breaking problems down, and the

8
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.590
four ways computer scientists think.

9
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.580
Computational thinking is a way of solving

10
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.780
problems that computers made famous.

11
00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:30.710
It has four parts.

12
00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.910
Decomposition, pattern recognition,

13
00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.210
abstraction and algorithms.

14
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.020
You and your pupils use them already;

15
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.260
naming them helps you teach them.

16
00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.960
Decomposition means breaking a big

17
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.600
problem into smaller ones.

18
00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:49.270
Planning a school sports day is

19
00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:50.730
too big to do at once.

20
00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.730
Break it into the races, the

21
00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.830
timetable, the equipment and the prizes.

22
00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.760
Each small part is easy to

23
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:00.680
solve on its own.

24
00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.230
Pattern recognition means spotting

25
00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.370
what is the same, like the loops

26
00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:07.530
in the robot’s program.

27
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.920
Abstraction means keeping

28
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.240
only what matters.

29
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.260
A bus map leaves out the shops and

30
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.100
trees, and keeps the stops.

31
00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:18.750
It’s simpler, and more useful.

32
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.680
You can find these four in every subject.

33
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.640
In maths, decompose a long word problem.

34
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.980
In science, spot the pattern in

35
00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:31.040
a week of temperatures.

36
00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:34.070
In language, a summary is an abstraction.

37
00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.190
In PE, the rules of a game

38
00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:38.010
are an algorithm.

39
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:39.880
Quick check!

40
00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:42.970
A pupil draws a map of the way to

41
00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.890
school with only the turns and the

42
00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:47.590
landmarks, nothing else.

43
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:49.330
Which part of

44
00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.130
computational thinking is that?

45
00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:53.310
Abstraction.

46
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.260
The map leaves out the houses and

47
00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.260
the colours, and keeps what you

48
00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.480
need to find your way.

49
00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.790
Good abstractions are everywhere: a

50
00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.910
timetable, a register, a recipe card.

51
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:08.370
Back to guess my number.

52
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.360
Both ways are algorithms, and

53
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:12.480
both find the number.

54
00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:15.590
One by one can take a hundred guesses.

55
00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.080
Halving the range each time

56
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.600
never needs more than seven.

57
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.650
Same job, very different work.

58
00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.130
Play it in class.

59
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.580
One pupil thinks of a number; the class

60
00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.800
guesses, and hears only higher or lower.

61
00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:33.920
Count the guesses for each

62
00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.200
strategy on the board.

63
00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.600
Pupils discover halving for

64
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.280
themselves, and that’s the best

65
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.400
way to learn an algorithm.

66
00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:44.150
In the app, open Guess the number.

67
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.920
With one to a hundred, press only

68
00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.260
Try the middle: you never need

69
00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.320
more than seven guesses.

70
00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.260
Then race one by one against halving,

71
00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.200
with numbers up to a thousand.

72
00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.120
Your turn.

73
00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.800
Which of these is decomposition:

74
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.220
splitting a class play into scenes,

75
00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.560
costumes and music, or drawing a

76
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.440
seating plan with only names?

77
00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:11.730
Splitting the play.

78
00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:14.870
Remember the four parts: decomposition

79
00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.850
breaks it down, patterns spot what

80
00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.570
repeats, abstraction keeps what matters,

81
00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.930
and algorithms give the steps.

82
00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:26.090
And some algorithms do the same

83
00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.990
job with far less work.

84
00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:30.380
Try it in class tomorrow:

85
00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.040
play Guess my number.

86
00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:35.330
Count the guesses for one by one and

87
00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.670
for halving, write both on the board,

88
00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.290
and ask: why does halving win?

89
00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:42.710
Then name the four parts

90
00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:43.630
in today’s lesson.
